t Collins's incumbency edge"], "rationale": "Nearly every fall poll shows Jackson slightly ahead or tied, and Maine's Democratic-leaning electorate plus a favorable midterm environment offset Collins's incumbency advantage. Raters and prediction markets both point to a small Democratic edge, though the latest surveys have tightened to even.", "watch": "A late shift toward Collins among independents, or a turnout edge in Maine's more rural second district, could flip the result.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race between Republican incumbent Susan Collins and Democrat Troy Jackson is one of the closest contests of 2026. Polls this fall show Jackson holding a slim edge or running even, and final-week surveys from Trafalgar and Beacon show the two tied. Jackson has led or matched Collins in nearly every public poll, though none shows either candidate breaking away.\n\nCollins retains the advantages of a long incumbency and more cash on hand, while Jackson has out-raised her overall and outside spending is roughly balanced. Maine's Democratic-leaning electorate and a midterm environment favoring Democrats offset some of Collins's traditional strength. Expert raters are split between Tossup and Lean Democratic, and prediction markets give Jackson roughly a 59 percent chance. A narrow Democratic edge is the best single estimate, but the race could readily tip the other way.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6668, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6495, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-